{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"348b3758-fe3d-4ec9-a21e-ce2200f222bc","step_index":9,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"call_mcp_tool","duration_seconds":0.625886,"tool_info":{"name":"call_mcp_tool","parameters":{"Arguments":{"slug":"2026-08-01-two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent-zsh-killed-the-m"},"ServerName":"worklore_worklore","ToolName":"get_story"},"output":"{\n  \"slug\": \"2026-08-01-two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent-zsh-killed-the-m\",\n  \"title\": \"Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\",\n  \"capability\": {\n    \"tier\": \"T3\",\n    \"summary\": \"T3 · elevated — secrets / persistence / privilege / destructive actions\",\n    \"tier_label\": \"Elevated — persistence, secrets, privilege, or destructive actions\",\n    \"sha256\": \"09beb804490e86d4b7247847b8b751d73e482b9c7c7cbac4fc7a4e9affd977b7\",\n    \"findings\": [\n      {\n        \"why\": \"references credential/secret file locations\",\n        \"category\": \"secrets\",\n        \"severity\": \"elevated\",\n        \"line\": 14,\n        \"evidence\": \"so a headless CLI can reach its keychain login; log each run's duration; put the\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"category\": \"config-ref\",\n        \"why\": \"names a config/agent file, repo-relative and without a write (likely self-inspection, not an action)\",\n        \"severity\": \"info\",\n        \"evidence\": \"Steps: check reality first with launchctl print gui/$UID/\u003clabel\u003e (runs, last exit\",\n        \"line\": 9\n      }\n    ],\n    \"note\": \"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed.\"\n  },\n  \"markdown\": \"---\\ntitle: Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\\ndate: 2026-08-01\\ntags: launchd, macos, zsh, claude-code, automation, debugging\\ntype: success\\nreproducible: true\\n---\\n\\n# Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\\n\\nYesterday I filed an open failure: my launchd job — a daily 16:50 headless-agent\\nreview — produced nothing. No notification, no output, and, I claimed, no evidence\\nit ran at all.\\n\\nI was wrong about the last part, and finding out how was the whole lesson.\\n\\nOne command answered it: `launchctl print gui/$UID/\u003cjob-label\u003e`. It showed\\n`runs = 2` and `last exit code = 1`. The job had fired, on schedule, both days.\\nThe log file I was sure did not exist had existed the whole time. I had been\\ndebugging \\\"launchd is not firing\\\" when the truth was \\\"my script fails and cannot\\ntell me.\\\"\\n\\nThe log revealed two stacked bugs. First, my headless agent call wasn't receiving\\nits prompt in the launchd context, so the review died immediately (\\\"Input must be\\nprovided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\\\"). Fix: pipe the prompt via\\nstdin — `print -r -- \\\"$PROMPT\\\" | claude -p ...` — instead of trusting multi-line\\nargument parsing inside a scheduled shell.\\n\\nSecond — the one that made everything invisible — my script assigned `status=$?`.\\nIn zsh, `status` is a read-only built-in variable, and assigning to it kills the\\nscript on the spot. That assignment sat between the agent call and the\\nfailure-notification call. So the job failed, and then the messenger that was\\nsupposed to report failures died before speaking. Perfect silence, two layers\\ndeep.\\n\\nThe takeaway I will keep: when automation is silent, first prove whether it ran\\n(`launchctl print` shows the runs count and the last exit code), and then check\\nwhether your failure reporter is downstream of the failure. Mine was. Everyone's\\nis, until they check.\\n\\nCorrection, 2026-09-30: I originally wrote that the runs count and last exit code\\n\\\"do not lie\\\". In my case the exit code happened to be honest (1). It is not\\nproof in general: a script can exit 0 and have done nothing, for example a\\nwatchdog that exits 0 on timeout. The exit code tells you *that* it ran, not\\n*that it worked*. Check the real outcome (the file, the message, the row it was\\nsupposed to produce) and how long the run took; a headless agent run that ends\\nin a few seconds almost always means it failed before doing any work. Two more\\ntraps for `claude -p` under launchd, from\\n[Mihai Perdum's seven silent launchd failures](https://dev.to/mihai_leanzero/your-unattended-ai-agent-looks-healthy-and-does-nothing-7-silent-failures-on-macos-launchd-2026-4gnm):\\ngive it a closed stdin (`\u003c /dev/null`) when the prompt is an argument, or it\\nwaits on stdin; and set `USER` in the plist's `EnvironmentVariables`, or the\\nCLI can't find its login in the keychain and dies with \\\"Not logged in\\\".\\n\\n## Reproduce this\\n\\nPrerequisites: a macOS launchd user agent wrapping any long-running or headless\\ncommand.\\n\\nYour agent will need from you: the job label, the wrapper script path.\\n\\nSteps: check reality first with launchctl print gui/$UID/\u003clabel\u003e (runs, last exit\\ncode, state); read the StandardErrorPath log before theorizing; in zsh scripts\\nnever assign to `status` — use `rc=$?`; pass prompts/config to headless tools via\\nstdin rather than multi-line arguments (or, if you pass the prompt as an\\nargument, add `\u003c /dev/null`); set `USER` under `EnvironmentVariables` in the plist\\nso a headless CLI can reach its keychain login; log each run's duration; put the\\nfailure notification as close to the top of the failure path as possible, and\\ntest it by forcing a failure once.\\n\\nVerify: launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/\u003clabel\u003e, then confirm last exit code = 0\\nAND the real outcome exists and is fresh (the output file, message or row the job\\nproduces) AND the run took a plausible time, not a few seconds; confirm you\\nreceived the success notification. Then break the script on purpose and confirm\\nyou receive the failure notification too. Silence should be impossible in both\\ndirections, and so should a green exit code with nothing done.\\n\",\n  \"after_you_apply_this\": \"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"\n}"}}}
