startup so the change persists across sessions\",\n        \"severity\": \"elevated\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"severity\": \"local\",\n        \"why\": \"writes or deletes files\",\n        \"category\": \"filewrite\",\n        \"line\": 51,\n        \"evidence\": \"# believe a CLAUDE.md's promise that its tools are GNU until `sed -i` or `date -d` fails\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"line\": 24,\n        \"why\": \"writes or deletes files\",\n        \"category\": \"filewrite\",\n        \"evidence\": \"Then a before/after in fresh headless sessions, with the same five commands that had failed for me. Before: all five failed, and one worse than failed: `sed -i -e 's/old/new/' f.txt` exited 0 and left\",\n        \"severity\": \"local\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"line\": 108,\n        \"evidence\": \"# and nothing else: it has the portable forms in CLAUDE.md. The developer gets a setup\",\n        \"why\": \"names a config/agent file, repo-relative and without a write (likely self-inspection, not an action)\",\n        \"severity\": \"info\",\n        \"category\": \"config-ref\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"why\": \"names a config/agent file, repo-relative and without a write (likely self-inspection, not an action)\",\n        \"line\": 5,\n        \"category\": \"config-ref\",\n        \"severity\": \"info\",\n        \"evidence\": \"# .claude/settings.json sets CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL to a bare `bash`, so the Bash tool runs\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"why\": \"names a config/agent file, repo-relative and without a write (likely self-inspection, not an action)\",\n        \"evidence\": \"# comes from `CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL` in the `env` block of a settings.json, read before the\",\n        \"category\": \"config-ref\",\n        \"line\": 7,\n        \"severity\": \"info\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"note\": \"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed.\"\n  },\n  \"markdown\": \"---\\ntitle: Why my agent's shell commands kept failing on a Mac: 54 errors counted, then fixed\\ndate: 2026-09-30\\ntags: [claude-code, macos, zsh, bash, gnu-coreutils, shell]\\ntype: success\\nstatus: n/a\\nreproducible: true\\nvisibility: public\\nstack: macOS (Apple Silicon) · Claude Code · Homebrew\\nagent: Claude Code\\nmodel: claude-opus-5-5\\n---\\n\\n# Why my agent's shell commands kept failing on a Mac: 54 errors counted, then fixed\\n\\nPlain version first: my coding agent writes shell commands for Linux, and my Mac is not Linux. Its shell is zsh, not bash, and its `sed`, `date` and `stat` are the old BSD versions, not the GNU ones the agent writes for. So a share of its commands fail, it retries, and I pay for both attempts.\\n\\nI counted instead of guessing. Across 21 of my Claude Code sessions in one project: 44 `(eval):1: no matches found` errors (zsh refusing an unquoted `--include=*.md` before grep even runs), 6 `illegal option` errors from BSD `date` and `stat`, and 4 BSD `sed` parse errors. One of them happened while I was reading about the fix.\\n\\nThe fix is [Morten Larsen's claude-code-macos](https://github.com/Baune8D/claude-code-macos), explained in [his article](https://dev.to/baunegaard/why-claude-code-keeps-writing-shell-commands-that-fail-on-your-mac-3bea): two settings that make Claude Code run bash, plus a SessionStart hook that puts Homebrew's GNU tools first on the PATH, only for the agent. My own terminal keeps the macOS tools.\\n\\nBefore installing a stranger's hooks I ran [skill-xray](https://github.com/worklore/skill-xray) on the repo. It said T4, the highest tier, which looked alarming. Reading the files settled it: the scanner had matched prose in the README describing how Claude Code itself re-launches its binary as ugrep. The hooks append one `export PATH=` line to the file Claude Code provides for that purpose and print a hint if something is missing. Nothing else.\\n\\nThen a before/after in fresh headless sessions, with the same five commands that had failed for me. Before: all five failed, and one worse than failed: `sed -i -e 's/old/new/' f.txt` exited 0 and left a stray `f.txt-e` behind. After: all five did what they say.\\n\\nTwo things I had predicted wrong. I expected my API keys to vanish, because they live in `~/.zshenv` and bash never reads it. They didn't: the agent's shell inherits Claude Code's own environment, which came from my zsh terminal. And the habit that does break is mine: I had been writing the BSD form `sed -i '' 's/a/b/' file`, and GNU sed rejects it. At least it fails loudly now instead of silently.\\n\\n## Reproduce this\\nPrerequisites: a Mac with Homebrew and Claude Code; `jq` (for the hooks' hints).\\nYour agent will need from you: permission to edit `~/.claude/settings.json` (it adds an `env` block and a SessionStart hook group; back it up first) and to run `brew install`.\\nSteps:\\n1. Measure first. Count past failures in your own transcripts: `grep -ho '(eval):1: no matches found' ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl | wc -l`, the same for `illegal option -- ` and `sed: 1: `.\\n2. Run skill-xray (https://github.com/worklore/skill-xray, `python3 scanner/scan.py \u003crepo\u003e`) on https://github.com/Baune8D/claude-code-macos, then read every hook it would run. Treat the tier as a reason to read, not as a verdict.\\n3. `brew install bash coreutils findutils gawk gnu-sed gnu-tar gnu-which grep`\\n4. Add `\\\"env\\\": {\\\"CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL\\\": \\\"bash\\\", \\\"SHELL\\\": \\\"bash\\\"}` to `~/.claude/settings.json`, and a SessionStart group running the three hooks from the repo's `.claude/hooks/` (`put-gnu-tools-on-path.sh`, `warn-shell-not-bash.sh`, `warn-old-bash.sh`) by absolute path. Or install the repo as a plugin and add only the `env` block by hand; a plugin can't set it.\\n5. Start a NEW session; settings are read at session start.\\n6. Stop writing BSD-only forms: `sed -i ''` fails under GNU sed. Use `sed -i 's/a/b/' file`.\\nVerify: in a fresh headless session (`claude -p \\\"...\\\" --allowedTools Bash \u003c /dev/null`), have the agent run each as its own Bash call: `grep -rn x --include=*.md docs`, `sed -i -e 's/old/new/' f.txt \u0026\u0026 ls`, `date -d yesterday +%F`, `stat -c %s f.txt`, `ls */a.md nomatch*.txt`, and `echo \\\"$BASH_VERSION\\\"; sed --version | head -1`. All should work (the last `ls` reports the one missing file instead of aborting), no `f.txt-e` appears, bash is 5.x and sed is GNU. Your API keys should still be set: print their length, never their value.\\n\",\n  \"after_you_apply_this\": \"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"\n}"}}}
